<Gooey>
  <!-- THE SLIDE SURFACE — one Canvas, patched over the wysiwyg editor's
       own DESIGNER pane.

       This file is a patch_markup FRAGMENT, not a page: its root child
       carries Name="EditorArea", which is the address of the pane it
       replaces in a running apps/wysiwyg. That is the whole trick of
       this talk — the slides are not a slideshow program, they are the
       editor's centre pane with something else in it, and the editor
       keeps running underneath.

       NOTHING HERE IS SLIDE CONTENT. Every visible string is a binding to
       a Deck.* property, and those properties do not exist in the wysiwyg
       binary — they are registered at runtime, from outside, over MCP
       (see present.sh). Advancing a slide is therefore one set_value per
       field and no rebuild at all: the paint nodes that read the changed
       property repaint, and nothing else on the screen does. Slide 2 is
       about exactly that mechanism, so the deck has to be built this way
       or the slide is a lie.

       THE TOUR-OVERLAY EFFECT is three layers of ordinary components, not
       an effect:

         Deck.Ghost   the editor's own markup, in `dim`, across the full
                      canvas — the scrim.
         the Border   a lighter Background painted over the scrim — the
                      spotlight. It pre-clears its own bounds, so the
                      scrim shows through around it and not under it.
         Deck.Spot    callouts in `warn`/`ok` that point OUT of the card
         Deck.Arrow   at real editor chrome — the activity rail above, the
         Deck.Blurb   status bar below. They are leaves on the same
         Deck.Meme    Canvas, so each clears only its own rect.

       The callout rows are chosen to miss the scrim's own text: rows 3,
       37, 39, 43 and 47-48. Ghost lines at those indices are left blank
       in the Deck.Ghost value rather than being clipped here — a leaf
       painted over another leaf wins the cell, and the result reads as
       corruption. Keeping them apart is the narration's job, not the
       layout's, which is why NARRATION.md ships the ghost text.

       KNOWN COSMETIC DEFECT, and it is visible on every slide: the body
       text inside the card renders on the terminal's default background
       rather than the card's. Border fills its interior and the leaf
       pre-clear inherits that fill (composer.go:263), but Text.Render
       then writes each glyph cell with its own render.Style
       (components/text.go:36 -> render/cell.go:59), and none of the
       registered styles set a Bg. So Style.Bg is the zero value, the
       glyph cells get the terminal default, and only the padding keeps
       the card colour. There is no markup-side fix: styles come from the
       host's ctx.Styles, in Go. -->
  <Canvas Name="EditorArea" Background="{{.Deck.Bg}}">

    <!-- The scrim. Full-canvas, dim, and deliberately DENSE: it is the
         editor's real markup, and it reads as the thing the card is
         sitting on top of. A sparse version was tried and looks like an
         empty screen with a box on it. -->
    <Text Canvas.Left="0" Canvas.Top="0" Style="dim">{{.Deck.Ghost}}</Text>

    <!-- Callout pointing UP-LEFT, at the activity rail (rows 0-7, cols
         0-3). Row 3 sits level with the rail's middle. -->
    <Text Canvas.Left="0" Canvas.Top="3" Style="warn">{{.Deck.Spot}}</Text>

    <!-- The spotlight. Title carries the slide number, so the kicker is
         chrome rather than another line of body. -->
    <Border Canvas.Left="14" Canvas.Top="6" Width="110" Height="30"
            Background="{{.Deck.Card}}" Style="sel" Title="{{.Deck.Kicker}}">
      <VStack Gap="1">
        <Text Bold="true" Style="title">{{.Deck.Title}}</Text>
        <Text>{{.Deck.Body}}</Text>
      </VStack>
    </Border>

    <!-- Below the card: an arrow, the mechanism note, and the joke. Three
         separate leaves rather than one block, so a slide can change the
         punchline without repainting the explanation. -->
    <Text Canvas.Left="16" Canvas.Top="37" Style="warn">{{.Deck.Arrow}}</Text>
    <Text Canvas.Left="16" Canvas.Top="39" Style="ok">{{.Deck.Blurb}}</Text>
    <Text Canvas.Left="16" Canvas.Top="43" Style="dim">{{.Deck.Meme}}</Text>

    <!-- The footer rail: position, progress, and a callout aimed at the
         REAL status bar on the row below (grpc + mcp addresses). The
         ProgressBar is a live component bound to an int, not drawn text —
         it is the smallest possible proof that the deck is a gooey tree
         and not a rendered image. -->
    <Text Canvas.Left="14" Canvas.Top="47" Style="dim">{{.Deck.Pos}}</Text>
    <ProgressBar Canvas.Left="24" Canvas.Top="47" Width="60" Value="{{.Deck.Pct}}"/>
    <Text Canvas.Left="88" Canvas.Top="47" Style="ok">{{.Deck.Hint}}</Text>
    <Text Canvas.Left="88" Canvas.Top="48" Style="warn">{{.Deck.Line1}}</Text>

  </Canvas>
</Gooey>
